import { PracticalCollectionCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> PracticalCollectionCopy = PracticalCollectionCopy {
  summary: "head returns an array’s first element as Maybe. An empty array returns Nothing; an existing first element, including an empty string or 0, returns Just.",
  taskTitle: "Read the first label, even when the list is empty",
  purpose: "Display the first label in a list, or missing when the list itself is empty. The important distinction is a first label that is blank versus no labels at all. The example’s final two lines compare those cases.",
  typescriptBefore: "In TypeScript, read labels[0] and check === undefined for absence. This is already concise and does not need a separate length check. Avoid a truthiness check so that the empty string remains present. The first five lines also compare indexed access using the same display function.",
  benefit: "Seseragi’s head needs no position argument and returns either Just with the first element or Nothing for an empty array. Handle the two Maybe cases directly instead of separating a length check from the read.",
  setup: "items and json are import aliases for std/array and std/json. The square brackets create labels as Array<String>. The call items.head labels takes one array argument; items.empty<String> () creates an empty array of String values.",
  after: [
    "describe accepts Maybe<String> and returns String. In its match, Just label extracts the existing string, while Nothing handles the absence of an element. Both branches return display text.",
    "The second-to-last call gets the empty string inside Just. json.encodeString adds quotation marks, producing present: \"\". The final call uses an empty array and displays missing.",
    "The earlier get calls check positions 0 and 1, a negative position, an out-of-range position, and an empty array. head serves the case where you want position 0. Both return Maybe, so the same describe function handles their results.",
  ],
  details: [
    "head reads the first element. It does not skip the empty string to find Review. A first element equal to 0 or False also returns Just. To look for the first element that meets a condition, use find.",
    "Just \"\" and Nothing are different values. Do not treat the whole result of head as a String; match it and use the contained value. The example displays missing for absence, but your application can choose its own message.",
    "head does not shorten the array or change its elements. Reading the first value with head differs from getting the remaining array after skipping a leading count with drop. For a List input, use head from std/list.",
  ],
